sed  the same instrumentation from our test  runner  very quickly with  honeycombs bubble up functionality  became very clear where the problems  were coming from  git lfs issues  popped up on a third of the qa  environment fleet and this was slowing  down the entire system  discovery was a simple query now tracing  platform on what doesn't look like  everything else  and within two hours  um  ci pipelines were flowing again and  engineers could do their work  over the next few months this led to the  first of many targeted investments for  ci tracing  here's a sample  of the shared dimensions we created for  users and developers in ci to make  queries in honeycomb legible and more  accessible  these dimensions were stubbed early in  our library and instrumented with a few  clients  since then various teams have extended  and reused these dimensions for their  use cases  and this graphic shows a very very high  level zoom out for individual traces and  what dimensions they might use in ci  in a single trace  example tests it might capture all the  data shown in the rightmost column you  can then track earlier related traces  for all the those shared dimensions  elsewhere  um  you could query to find like commit  related tests and build traces  to see if we're seeing build failures or  other test failures due to code issue on  master  like and like let's go back to the root  challenge developer frustration across  lac was increasing due to flaky test  runs over the last several years  flaky test runs was one of the top  reported issues in developer surveys  for a few quarters already  in 2020 by mid-2020 automation teams  across slack had a daily 30-minute  triage session to triage and focus on  the flakiest tests  automation team leads hesitated to  introduce any additional variants on how  we use cyprus and and testing framework  the belief  was that test flakiness was from the  test code itself  yet  there wasn't great progress  i typically work on the infrasight 